        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-43" parent="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-35" style="text;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=4;spacingRight=4;overflow=hidden;rotatable=0;points=[[0,0.5],[1,0.5]];portConstraint=eastwest;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;" value="&lt;i&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 10px;&quot;&gt;final&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&amp;nbsp;version: long" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="28" width="520" y="222" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-44" parent="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-35" style="text;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=4;spacingRight=4;overflow=hidden;rotatable=0;points=[[0,0.5],[1,0.5]];portConstraint=eastwest;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;" value="&lt;font&gt;toString(): String&lt;/font&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="28" width="520" y="250" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-45" parent="1" style="html=1;shadow=0;dashed=0;shape=mxgraph.bootstrap.rrect;rSize=5;strokeColor=#999999;strokeWidth=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;fontColor=#6C767D;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;spacing=10;" value="A record is an ordered set of tuples. Each tuple represents a different state of the record. A record can be created without any state, but beware that records themself are not persisted, they are pure logical entities." vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="92" width="440" x="2000" y="3000" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-46" parent="1" style="html=1;shadow=0;dashed=0;shape=mxgraph.bootstrap.rrect;rSize=5;strokeColor=#999999;strokeWidth=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;fontColor=#6C767D;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;spacing=10;" value="The data number uniquely identifies a data tuple. It is stringified into a URN (Unique Resource Name) with the syntax:&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;urn:here:naksha:{database}:{catalog}:{collection}:{record}[:{version}]&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="109" width="520" x="3090" y="3030" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-47" parent="1" style="html=1;shadow=0;dashed=0;shape=mxgraph.bootstrap.rrect;rSize=5;strokeColor=#999999;strokeWidth=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;fontColor=#6C767D;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;spacing=10;" value="A tuple is a specific immutable state of a record. It is technically kept in JBON format on the heap, so it is a pure single byte-array.&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;If a storage extracts data from a tuple and stores it in a dedicated place, for example dedicated table columns, it will replace the values in the DataMap with data references, and store the values behind the JBON in the JBON extension area. The strings will be encoded as JBON strings, so they can be interned using NakshaData from lib-data.&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="230" width="440" x="2550" y="2850" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-48" parent="1" style="swimlane;fontStyle=0;childLayout=stackLayout;horizontal=1;startSize=26;fillColor=#dae8fc;horizontalStack=0;resizeParent=1;resizeParentMax=0;resizeLast=0;collapsible=1;marginBottom=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" value="DataTupleBuilder" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="138" width="440" x="1980" y="3428" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-49" parent="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-48" style="text;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=4;spacingRight=4;overflow=hidden;rotatable=0;points=[[0,0.5],[1,0.5]];portConstraint=eastwest;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#e1d5e7;strokeColor=#9673a6;" value="DataTupleBuilder(DataRecord record)" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="28" width="440" y="26" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-50" parent="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-48" style="text;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=4;spacingRight=4;overflow=hidden;rotatable=0;points=[[0,0.5],[1,0.5]];portConstraint=eastwest;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;" value="&lt;i&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 10px;&quot;&gt;final&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&amp;nbsp;record: DataRecord" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="28" width="440" y="54" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-51" parent="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-48" style="text;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=4;spacingRight=4;overflow=hidden;rotatable=0;points=[[0,0.5],[1,0.5]];portConstraint=eastwest;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;strokeColor=#666666;fontColor=#333333;" value="... setter/getter for all attributes of the data tuple, plus custom values" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="28" width="440" y="82" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-52" parent="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-48" style="text;align=left;verticalAlign=top;spacingLeft=4;spacingRight=4;overflow=hidden;rotatable=0;points=[[0,0.5],[1,0.5]];portConstraint=eastwest;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;" value="&lt;font&gt;build(): DataTuple&lt;/font&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="28" width="440" y="110" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-53" parent="1" style="html=1;shadow=0;dashed=0;shape=mxgraph.bootstrap.rrect;rSize=5;strokeColor=#999999;strokeWidth=1;fillColor=#FFFFFF;fontColor=#6C767D;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;spacing=10;" value="A tuple is a specific immutable state of a record." vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="80" width="440" x="1980" y="3348" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-54" parent="1" style="html=1;shadow=0;dashed=0;shape=mxgraph.bootstrap.rrect;rSize=5;strokeColor=#666666;strokeWidth=1;fillColor=#f5f5f5;fontColor=#333333;whiteSpace=wrap;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;spacingLeft=10;fontSize=14;spacing=10;" value="This is the Storage Abstraction Layer. All data is generally assume to be GeoJSON compatible. The concept is that data is stored in databases, which contain catalogs, which contain collections, which contain records, which persist out of tuples. A tuple represents a unique immutable state of a record. All states of a record form a temporal chain, with the first state being the creation of the record, and the last state representing the destruction of the record. Beware that the last state is called tombstone state, and can hold changes. So, when deleting a record, the state can be changed as part of the delete operation, because the tombstone (delete) states are readable from a collection. The deleted state is a valid state, signaling the end of live of a record. Most queries do ignore these delete states, but they can be included into reads (this is very necessary for views). Actually, there is another special operation called PURGE which finally delete the tombstone (delete) state&amp;nbsp;from the HEAD of the collection, moving it into the HISTORY. Therefore, after a PURGE the record is no longer reachable through the HEAD of a collection. Executing a PURGE without an upfront DELETE make this a DELETE and PURGE operation. In that case the DELETE state is directly written into HISTORY. If no data changes are done, the DELETE operation will just be a copy of the latest state of the record.&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;Another concept is that databases can be stored in multiple storages. Think of this like keeping backups, so there can be multiple storages hosting the same database. The problem is that there should be one main database. At least at runtime, only one of the storages will be able to create new states, all others are treated as replicas (aka caches) and only keep copies of tuple. So, creation of new records or states (tuple) requires a link to the owner storage.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;There are supportive libraries like `lib-heapcache` which implements a storage to cache data on the JVM heap, and there are implementations like `lib-psql` to store data in a PostgresQL database. Other planned libraries are `lib-sqlite`, `lib-memcache`, `lib-redis`, ... The concept for caching storages is simple, they first check in their own cache, if not found, they forward the request to the next storage.&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div&gt;One big pro of this design is that storages do not care about memory management at all. They only do their job, the rest is taken care of helper libraries.&lt;/div&gt;" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="385" width="1090" x="2000" y="1435" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="A99nmO6RLz12Ya_JZG_t-62" parent="1" style="swimlane;fontStyle=0;childLayout=stackLayout;horizontal=1;startSize=26;fillColor=#dae8fc;horizontalStack=0;resizeParent=1;resizeParentMax=0;resizeLast=0;collapsible=1;marginBottom=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;strokeColor=#6c8ebf;" value="&lt;i&gt;&lt;font style=&quot;font-size: 10px;&quot;&gt;final&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/i&gt;&amp;nbsp;DataCollection" vertex="1">
          <mxGeometry height="194" width="440" x="1990" y="2530" as="geometry" />
